      "latency_ms": 1856.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 834,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. The transitions between these phases occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature rises, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires a lot of energy to evaporate, driving weather and cooling mechanisms (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between water molecules creates a “skin” that allows insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater in oceans). Freshwater (rivers, lakes, groundwater, ice caps) makes up only ~2.5 % of total water, and most of that is locked in glaciers or deep underground. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and DNA replication all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular cytoplasm are water‑based fluids that move nutrients, waste, and signals.<br>• **Structural** – water provides turgor pressure in plant cells and contributes to the shape of many organisms. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture (irrigation), sanitation, power generation (hydropower, cooling), manufacturing (solvent, cleaning), food processing, recreation, etc. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • **Climate regulation** – oceans store and redistribute heat.<br>• **Weather cycles** – evaporation, condensation, and precipitation drive the water cycle.<br>• **Ecosystem support** – habitats like rivers, lakes, wetlands, and oceans depend on water’s presence and quality. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n- **Polarity + hydrogen bonding:** Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with neighbors, creating a dynamic, cohesive network that gives water its high boiling point, surface tension, and solvent abilities.\n- **Broad liquid temperature range:** Because of hydrogen bonding, water remains liquid over a wide temperature span (0 °C–100 °C at 1 atm), which is ideal for supporting life.\n- **Self‑regulating pH:** Pure water undergoes auto‑ionization (2 H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻), giving it a neutral pH of 7 at 25 °C, a useful baseline for biochemical systems.\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose unique hydrogen‑bonding network gives it extraordinary physical and chemical properties. These properties make it an unparalleled solvent, a regulator of temperature, a participant in the planet’s water cycle, and the medium in which virtually all known biological processes occur."
